  "notes": "Drawn from 2022–2024 left-side photos. The two cheatlines meet near the nose tip and diverge aft with a silver gap between them; their exact heights at the tail cone, and whether they continue under the belly, aren't clear in the photos. The right side and the belly weren't checked. Re-checked 2026-11-15 on N915NN (left, 2022-12-18, near level), measured against the doors: 'American' x 0.208-0.330 and the Flight Symbol at 0.195, further aft than first drawn (as on the AirCal and Reno Air 737s); 'TWA' on the fin with baseline 0.33, cap 0.14; the cheatlines' sweeps drawn as curves. The title's cap height measures about 0.67 m (0.33), a little over the 0.24 drawn: left as noise. 'TWA' re-measured 2026-11-15 on the level 2017 photo (N915NN at Miami): at its baseline the lettering runs from x 0.21 (the T's crossbar, the italic's left end) to 0.91 of the chord, so its T clears the red panel's leading edge at the cap height on the swept 737 fin; it had started at 0.10, the T poking past the panel's edge. The right side isn't photographed: the title covers the same stretch there, its A close to the panel's leading edge. Nose re-checked 2026-11-27 (N915NN from ahead at O'Hare 2017-02-14 and front three-quarter 2024-08-28): the two cheatlines run on level under the cockpit and cross the nose front just under the windscreen, meeting the other side's; they had been drawn falling to the nose tip under a silver nose cap."
